Ladybird caps each serialized history state at 16 MiB, and gives a
tab’s whole session history a 1 GiB budget for state, enforced in the
UI process. Gecko also caps each state at 16 MiB, and throws past it,
in nsDocShell::AddState() (the browser.history.maxStateObjectSize
pref). WebKit’s History::updateAndCheckStateObjectQuota() instead
allows each main-frame document 64 MB of state in all, and throws a
QuotaExceededError past that. Blink sets no limit on a state’s size.
But Blink and Gecko both bound the total by keeping at most 50 session
history entries (Blink’s kMaxSessionHistoryEntries, and Gecko’s
browser.sessionhistory.max_entries pref), which Ladybird doesn’t do
yet, so its budget for state bounds the total instead.
